# Variables and PATH
> A variable holds text. Set it with NAME=value (no spaces around the equals sign) and read
> it with $NAME. `export` also hands it to the programs you start. AmigaDOS has the same
> idea in local and global variables (SetEnv, GetEnv).
? Set the variable GREETING to hello.
= [ "$GREETING" = hello ]
~ GREETING=hello
! GREETING=hello
? Print it.
= grep -q hello "$LEARN_OUT"
~ echo $GREETING
! echo $GREETING
? Export the variable TOOL with the value tutorial.
= [ "$TOOL" = tutorial ]
~ export TOOL=tutorial
! export TOOL=tutorial
> `$PATH` is the list of drawers where vsh looks for commands, written in Unix form with
> colons between the drawers (/UP-Term/bin:/gg/bin:/c by default). After it, vsh also
> looks in the Shell's own path (the AmigaDOS Path command).
? Print the PATH.
= grep -q / "$LEARN_OUT"
~ echo $PATH
! echo $PATH
